: \\n451: graphcalc solves this as a MIP over binaries `x, y` with `f = x + 2y`,\\n452: constraint `x v + y v + Σ_{u ∈ N(v)} y u ≥ 1` and exclusion `x v + y v ≤ 1`;\\n453: that is exactly the condition below. -/\\n454: def IsRomanDominatingFunction (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\\n455:     (f : V → Fin 3) : Prop := ∀ v : V, f v = 0 → ∃ u : V, G.Adj v u ∧ f u = 2\\n456: \\n457: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (f : V → Fin 3) :\\n458:     Decidable (IsRomanDominatingFunction G f) := by\\n459:   unfold IsRomanDominatingFunction; infer_instance\\n460: \\n461: def romanWeight (f : V → Fin 3) : ℕ := ∑ v : V, (f v : ℕ)\\n462: \\n463: /-- Total, not partial: the constant function `2` is always Roman dominating\\n464: (no vertex is labelled `0`), so a witness always exists. -/\\n465: def romanDominationNumber (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\\n466:   (((univ.filter (IsRomanDominatingFunc\\n<truncated 1402 bytes>\\n Decidable (IsDoubleRomanDominatingFunction G f) := by\\n489:   unfold IsDoubleRomanDominatingFunction; infer_instance\\n490: \\n491: def doubleRomanWeight (f : V → Fin 4) : ℕ := ∑ v : V, (f v : ℕ)\\n492: \\n493: /-- Total, for the same reason as `romanDominationNumber`: constant `3` works. -/\\n494: def doubleRomanDominationNumber (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\\n495:   (((univ.filter (IsDoubleRomanDominatingFunction G)).image\\n496:     doubleRomanWeight).min).getD 0\\n497: \\n498: /- ── radius ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -/\\n499: \\n500: /-- Vertices within `k` steps of `v`, by iterated neighbourhood expansion.\\n501: Distance is not available to the kernel directly (`SimpleGraph.dist` is defined\\n502: through `sInf` over walk lengths), so it is reconstructed as reachability. -/\\n503: def ballWithin (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ → V → Finset V\\n504:   | 0,       v => {v}\\n505:   | (k + 1), v =>\\n506:       let B := ballWithin G k v\\n507:       B ∪ B.biUnion (fun u => univ.filter (fun w => G.Adj u w))\\n508: \\n509: /-- Eccentricity of `v`: least `k` with every vertex within `k` steps. -/\\n510: def eccentricity (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (v : V) : ℕ :=\\n511:   minUpTo (Fintype.card V) (fun k => ballWithin G k v = univ)\\n512: \\n513: def radius (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\\n514:   ((univ.image (eccentricity G)).min).getD 0\\n515: \\n516: /-- Undefined on a disconnected graph, where some vertex never reaches all of\\n517: `univ` and `minUpTo` would fall back to `n`. networkx raises there. -/\\n518: def HasRadius (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\\n519:   ∀ v : V, ballWithin G (Fintype.card V) v = univ\\n520: \\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\\n\",\"truncated_fields\":[\"content\"]}\n14: {\"step_index\":13,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T14:53:43Z\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"grep_search\",\"args\":{\"MatchPerLine\":\"true\",\"Query\":\"\\\"IsNontrivialClass\\\"\",\"SearchPath\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Search IsNontrivialClass\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Search IsNontrivialClass in reference\\\"\"}}]}\n15: {\"step_index\":14,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T14:53:50Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:53:50+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:53:50+02:00\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":272,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"def IsNontrivialClass (G : SimpleGraph V) : Prop := 2 ≤ order G\\\"}\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":274,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"instance (G : SimpleGraph V) : Decidable (IsNontrivialClass G) := by\\\"}\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":275,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"  unfold IsNontrivialClass; infer_instance\\\"}\"}\n16: {\"step_index\":15,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T14:53:50Z\",\"thinking\":\"**Defining Graph Properties**\\n\\nThe goal is to define several graph properties: `IsNontrivialClass`, `order`, `HasRadius`, `eccentricity`, `radius`, and `romanDominationNumber`.  `order` is defined using the cardinality of the vertex set. `HasRadius` indicates if a graph has a defined radius based on the ball centered at each vertex.\\n\\nFocusing on `eccentricity`, it's the minimum distance such that the ball centered at a vertex encompasses the entire vertex set.  `radius` is derived from the minimum eccentricity of all vertices. The definition of Roman domination number appears incomplete, it requires the condition after the arrow in `IsRomanDominatingFunction G f`.\\n\\n\\n**Evaluating the Conjecture**\\n\\nThe conjecture `radius + 1 <= roman_domination_number` is being evaluated for connected, nontrivial graphs. Considering the complete graph, star graph, and path graphs, initial tests suggest the conjecture holds.  Path graph $P_4$ is examined to test the conjecture more rigorously, requiring an exploration of its roman domination number. The radius of $P_4$ is 2, thus radius + 1 is 3.\\n\\nConsidering whether the roman domination number of $P_4$ is less than or equal to 3.\\n\\n\\n**Determining Roman Domination Numbers**\\n\\nFor $P_4$, a '2' vertex can dominate a maximum of three vertices, suggesting a minimum Roman domination number of 3.  Placing a '2' at vertex 2 and a '1' at vertex 4 achieves this, thus $\\\\gamma_R(P_4) = 3$. This confirms radius + 1 ≤ 3.\\n\\nNext, examining $P_5$, a Roman domination number of 3 seems unattainable. Placing '2' on vertex 3 would leave vertices 1 and 5 uncovered, requiring additional weight. Placing '2' on vertex 2 leaves 4 and 5 uncovered, requiring a total weight of at least 4. This indicates $\\\\gamma_R(P_5) = 4$.\\n\\nConsidering $C_4$, a single '2' can only dominate 3 vertices, implying $\\\\gamma_R(C_4) \\\\ge 2$. However, since we can do weight 3.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing Roman Domination Example**\\n\\nExamining the $C_4$ cycle graph, a configuration assigning a value of '2' to one vertex, and '1' to another is valid, y\\n<truncated 656 bytes>\\nrio**\\n\\nConsidering the Roman domination number ($\\\\gamma_R = 3$), where a single '2' is assigned to one vertex. This arrangement implies the eccentricity of the vertex with the '2' assignment must be at most 1, thus the graph's radius is 1, and radius + 1 = 2 ≤ 3, thus satisfying the conjecture.\\n\\nConsidering $\\\\gamma_R = 4$, if there are two '2' vertices, all other vertices must be at distance at most 1 from at least one of these.  This would make the radius $\\\\le 1$ which would mean the radius + 1 would $\\\\le 2$, which is less than 4.\\n\\nIf we have two vertices with weight 2 each, and a radius of 2. It will satisfy radius + 1 <= 4, in which case the graph radius + 1 must be less than or equal to 4.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing Vertex Adjacency and Distance**\\n\\nIf two vertices, $c_1$ and $c_2$, are adjacent, the distance is 1, implying the eccentricity of $c_1$ is at most 2. If $c_1$ and $c_2$ are not adjacent, and they are connected, a path exists.\\n\\nIf a vertex $u$ is adjacent to both $c_1$ and $c_2$, their distance is 2; the eccentricity of $c_1$ is at most 3. If no common neighbor exists, consider whether a path between neighbors exists.\\n\\nA path $c_1 - u_1 - u_2 - c_2$ implies a distance of 3, where $u_1$ is a neighbor of $c_1$ and $u_2$ is a neighbor of $c_2$, and the vertices $c_1$, $u_1$, $u_2$, and $c_2$ have to be connected.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing Path Graph Properties**\\n\\nExamined a specific Roman dominating function for $P_6$, confirming its validity and deriving a Roman domination number of 4. Determined the radius of $P_6$ to be 3, satisfying the conjecture.\\n\\nNext, examining $P_7$, confirmed the radius to be 3.\\n\\n\\n**Evaluating Roman Domination of Path Graphs**\\n\\nDetermined that a Roman dominating function of weight less than or equal to 4 cannot dominate $P_7$, thus $\\\\gamma_R(P_7) \\\\ge 5$. Similarly, determined $\\\\gamma_R(P_8) \\\\ge 6$.\\n\\nFormulated general formulas: radius($P_n$) = $\\\\lceil (n-1)/2 \\\\rceil = \\\\lfloor n/2 \\\\rfloor$, and hypothesized $\\\\gamma_R(P_n) = \\\\lceil 2n/3 \\\\rceil$ for path graphs. Verified this formula through $n=8$.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"run_command\",\"args\":{\"CommandLine\":\"\\\"python3 -c \\\\\\\"import networkx; print('networkx available')\\\\\\\"\\\"\",\"Cwd\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9\\\"\",\"WaitMsBeforeAsync\":\"5000\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Check python environment\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Check if networkx is available\\\"\"}}],\"truncated_fields\":[\"thinking\"]}\n17: {\"step_index\":16,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T14:54:09Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:54:09+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:54:10+02:00\\n\\nThe command exited with code 1.\\nOutput:\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\r\\n  File \\\"<string>\\\", line 1, in <module>\\r\\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'networkx'\\r\\n\\n\"}\n18: {\"step_index\":17,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T14:54:10Z\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"find_by_name\",\"args\":{\"Pattern\":\"\\\"*.lean\\\"\",\"SearchDirec